  "about": {
    "@type": "Event",
    "name": "Operation Cast Lead",
    "startDate": "2008-12-27",
    "endDate": "39831",
    "description": "22-day Israeli military operation in Gaza Strip",
    "location": {
      "@type": "Place",
      "name": "Gaza Strip, Under Israeli blockade and occupation; governed by Hamas, Ongoing blockade since 2007; reconstruction incomplete",
      "geo": {
        "@type": "GeoCoordinates",
        "latitude": 31.5,
        "longitude": 34.47
      }
    }
  },
  "articleBody": "Operation Cast Lead began at 11:30 AM on December 27, 2008, when Israeli F-16 fighter jets and Apache helicopters simultaneously struck over 100 pre-planned targets across Gaza within 220 seconds. The opening attacks hit police stations—including during a graduation ceremony for new officers, killing 99 police personnel—government buildings, and Hamas infrastructure. By the end of the first day, at least 230 Palestinians were killed. The aerial bombardment continued for seven days before Israeli ground forces invaded Gaza from the north and east on January 3, 2009. The navy simultaneously shelled from offshore. Over 22 days, Israeli forces conducted what the UN investigation described as a deliberately disproportionate attack designed to punish, humiliate and terrorize a civilian population. The operation involved widespread destruction of civilian infrastructure including hospitals, schools, mosques, United Nations facilities, water installations, and sewage treatment plants. Multiple incidents documented by the UN mission and human rights organizations revealed patterns of violations: the Samouni family massacre where soldiers forced approximately 100 civilians into a building then shelled it killing 21; the Al-Daya family airstrike killing 22 civilians; several cases where soldiers shot civilians carrying white flags; and extensive use of white phosphorus munitions in densely populated areas. The Palestinian Centre for Human Rights documented 1,419 Palestinian deaths including 1,167 civilians, while B'Tselem reported 1,385 killed including 762 noncombatants and 318 minors.",
  "keywords": "Military Operation, historical, War Crimes; Crimes Against Humanity (UN Goldstone Report)",
